WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.809 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Two friends are checking numbered tickets for a
school event.

00:00:03.929 --> 00:00:06.108 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Almost every digit matches!

00:00:06.228 --> 00:00:08.089 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Which ticket comes first?

00:00:08.209 --> 00:00:14.027 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
A quick glance at the final digits could fool
us, so let’s investigate from the right starting
place.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.997 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Today, we’re learning to compare and order whole
numbers using place value.

00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:23.726 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You’ll be able to compare from the largest place
until I find a difference.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:27.489 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You’ll be able to use the symbols <, > and =
correctly.

00:00:27.867 --> 00:00:32.483 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You’ll be able to order numbers and explain your
choice using digit values.

00:00:32.833 --> 00:00:36.394 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Which number is larger when most of the digits
match?

00:00:36.514 --> 00:00:40.079 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Compare from the greatest place value, not from
the right.

00:00:40.199 --> 00:00:44.960 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
For whole numbers with no leading zeros, more
digits means a larger number.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:48.449 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
If the lengths match, compare one column at a
time.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.042 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Which ticket number is greater?

00:00:51.162 --> 00:00:53.927 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Point to the first place where the digits
differ.

00:00:54.047 --> 00:00:56.216 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
We can stop once we find it.

00:00:57.733 --> 00:01:06.523 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Compare four hundred and fifty six thousand,
seven hundred and eighty two, with four hundred
and fifty six thousand, seven hundred and twenty
eight.

00:01:06.643 --> 00:01:10.866 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Their hundred thousands, ten thousands,
thousands and hundreds match.

00:01:10.986 --> 00:01:14.435 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
We have not found a difference yet, so keep
moving right.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.863 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
At the tens place, eight is greater than two.

00:01:17.983 --> 00:01:19.105 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Stop there.

00:01:19.225 --> 00:01:29.998 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The first number is greater, so four hundred and
fifty six thousand, seven hundred and eighty two
is greater than four hundred and fifty six
thousand, seven hundred and twenty eight.

00:01:30.118 --> 00:01:32.905 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The ones cannot overturn that difference.

00:01:33.267 --> 00:01:36.228 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Try this pair before I show the comparison.

00:01:36.348 --> 00:01:41.752 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Will the many nines at the end decide the
answer, or will an earlier place decide?

00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:50.263 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Now compare three hundred and nine thousand,
nine hundred and fifty, with three hundred and
ten thousand and five.

00:01:50.383 --> 00:01:52.440 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The hundred thousands match.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.655 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
At ten thousands, zero is less than one.

00:01:55.775 --> 00:02:00.638 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Therefore three hundred and nine thousand, nine
hundred and fifty is smaller.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.271 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
To order several numbers, repeat the same
method.

00:02:04.391 --> 00:02:12.305 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Eighty nine thousand, nine hundred and ninety
nine comes before ninety thousand and one, which
comes before ninety thousand and ten.

00:02:12.425 --> 00:02:15.653 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Do not compare only the final three digits.

00:02:15.773 --> 00:02:18.278 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The greatest differing place decides.

00:02:18.633 --> 00:02:27.886 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Sam compares only the final three digits and
thinks eighty-nine thousand, nine hundred and
ninety-nine must be greater than ninety thousand
and one.

00:02:28.006 --> 00:02:30.525 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
That overlooks the ten-thousands!

00:02:30.645 --> 00:02:35.616 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Nine ten-thousands is greater than eight
ten-thousands, whatever follows.

00:02:35.967 --> 00:02:43.179 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Which is greater: 582,430 or 582,403?

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.710 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The first differing place is the tens.

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:57.540 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Three tens is greater than zero tens, so 582,430
is greater.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:06.384 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
As a supporting estimation check, rounding
eighty nine thousand, nine hundred and ninety
nine to the nearest thousand gives ninety
thousand.

00:03:06.504 --> 00:03:11.370 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
A rounded value is useful, but it does not
replace an exact comparison.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:17.189 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Recap: line up the places, start left, find the
first difference, and explain your order.

00:03:17.567 --> 00:03:21.954 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Our goal was to compare and order whole numbers
using place value.

00:03:22.074 --> 00:03:27.857 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You can now explain your strategy, check the
places carefully, and show why your answer makes
sense.

00:03:27.977 --> 00:03:31.590 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Choose a number of your own and explain it to
someone nearby.
